  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nFriday...Upper flow across the region shifts to a more west to \nsouthwest direction, as a large Pacific trough begins to strengthen \nalong the coastline of British Columbia and the Pacific NW. \nMeanwhile, another transient upper shortwave crosses the Four \nCorners region. Models indicate even more isolated activity for the \nmts during the afternoon, mainly tied to the Continental Divide, \nwhile temps across the eastern plains push upward to near 90 degrees \nfor some spots. By late Fri night, the Pacific system starts to \ndeepen and drop south along the West Coast, setting up for the \nweekend. Plan on highs in the 70s for the high valleys, and 80s to \nnear 90F for the plains.\n\nSaturday...Big changes in the upper pattern start to take place \nSaturday, as the Pacific low pressure trough digs even further south \nand absorbs the remnants of Hurricane Rachel. Deep southwest flow \naloft develops across the Four Corners, feeding this moisture into \nwestern CO and dramatically increasing precipitation chances and \ncoverage starting in the afternoon and lasting through the night. \nMeanwhile, Tropical Storm Simon starts to push north along the \nMexico coastline. Forecast QPF totals through Saturday total to \naround .4 to .5 of an inch across the southwest mts, while the rest \nof the Divide receives lesser amounts. While the high valleys will \ncool a couple degrees due to increasing cloud cover and precip \nchances, the eastern plains will remain hot. Look for highs in the \n60s to around 70F for the high valleys, and 80s to near 90F once \nagain for the plains.\n\nSunday and Monday...Strong Pacific low system stays anchored along \nthe CA coast both days, keeping strong southwest flow aloft over the \nFour Corners. The remnants of Simon slide north along the Mexico \ncoastline and eventually get absorbed with the flow aloft and pushed \ninto CO, continuing rainfall across all of the higher terrain but \nmainly focusing along the Continental Divide and the southern mts. \nForecast QPF for the sw mts for both days equals to around an inch \nand a half of additional water, so a Flash Flood Watch may need to \nbe considered on following shifts if the models remain consistent. \nAs for temps, high valleys will see highs in the 60s both days which \nis right around seasonal normals, while the plains remain hot and \nabove normal. A cold front late Monday evening across the eastern \nplains will bring a change to that, starting Tuesday.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...A very active weather pattern through mid \nweek, as the upper low finally moves onshore across CA and NV on \nTue, then across CO on Wed. This path will bring widespread precip \nchances to all of the forecast area on Tue, and possibly through \nWed. Look for significantly cooler temps as well, as the cold front \nlate Mon evening combines with extensive cloud cover and ongoing \nprecipitation. High temps both days will likely be in the 60s for \nthe high valleys, and 60s to around 70F for the plains. Tue and Wed \nwill be the time period for some snow across the mts as well, as \nsnow levels finally begin to lower. Moore",
